WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.060
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Dr. Aaron Carroll, who's a professor,

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:09.590
pediatrics at Indiana university school of
medicine and the Regan Streif Institute.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:14.259
I hope I'm pronouncing that correctly, who
writes about health research and policy at

00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:20.440
the New York times and his blog, the incidental
economist and has a YouTube show on these

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.260
topics called healthcare triage.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:23.460
So great to have you on today.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:24.720
Thanks for Ambien.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.369
So let's start our conversation about evaluating
different systems of health care and paying

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:31.369
for healthcare.

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:37.340
Maybe by establishing how we would measure
how well a system functions, what are the

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.770
key things that we should be thinking about?

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:44.860
Are they a of care quality of care and outcomes?

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:49.570
The cost per capita, like how do we even establish
the guidelines for figuring this out?

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.350
So we care about all of those things.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:55.470
So whenever we talk about a healthcare system
or any kind of healthcare forum, I always

00:00:55.470 --> 00:01:00.120
like to, to frame it in terms of what some
people refer to as the iron triangle, but

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.820
there are three aspects of a healthcare system.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:07.040
You always need to think about how much does
it cost, what's its access, like how is it,

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.850
is it cow, can you get the stuff that you
want or need?

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.980
And then what is the quality that it produces?

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:17.310
What makes it an iron triangle is that you
can make any one, maybe two of those things

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.990
better, but it often comes at the expense
of the third.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:24.520
I can make a system that's cheaper tomorrow,
but then probably it's either going to be

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.880
less quality or there won't be as much of
it.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.480
I can make a system universal tomorrow, meaning
everybody can have everything they want.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.280
Again, that's going to cost more or probably
quality of suffering and I can give you the

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.090
best system in the world, but then again that's
going to cost money or you know, access is

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.300
going to suffer in some way.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:44.560
So the problem is that we are always looking
for a silver bullet that does all three of

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.370
those things at the same time.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:47.610
It's just not going to happen.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:52.800
So most healthcare form that we want to consider,
we have to say, what are we trying to achieve

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.180
and what are we willing to give up in order
to get there?

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:56.700
What are the trade offs?

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.229
We don't engage in that.

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:04.240
Now, within those three domains, there's lots
of ways to measure a healthcare system with

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.380
respect to cost.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.670
Is it public costs or private costs?

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:08.759
How much is out of pocket?

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:12.260
How much are premiums, how much, uh, would
you still have to pay?

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.410
Even after that, how much would come out of
taxes?

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:19.390
It gets very complicated, but the United States
is an incredibly expensive system no matter

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:20.390
how you're breaking.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.390
Yeah.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:22.390
I want to get to that in a second.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:28.080
I mean, one of the things that I recently
read a UIE Reinhart's latest book where he,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.620
when he talks about the cost, he says something
which makes sense, but I think is often not

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.959
in the conversation that happens at least
in the media about health healthcare, which

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:41.610
is that as you point out, there is some total
cost and that can go up or down.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:45.730
What is the total cost to treat the average
person in a year?

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:51.459
And then oftentimes what we're really debating
is actually how should that total cost be

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:53.670
divided from the population.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:59.340
In other words, should we tax Richard people
more in order to pay or, or so on and so forth.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:05.190
Is that a part of the discussion that is maybe
not spoken about in those clear terms?

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:06.190
Sometimes.

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.450
I think it's part of, but it's not even all
of it.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:13.540
Uh, you know, part of it is that we think
we have a very, uh, private system and we

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:18.030
do compare it to some countries, but still
about two thirds of healthcare spending in

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.810
the United States is what we would consider
not private.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:27.000
It's public, meaning that it's, it's Medicare,
it's Medicaid, all of what your taxpayer were.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.680
Some other way, you know, government run.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:34.120
It's the VA, it's everyone that works for
a public institution but has private insurance.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.240
Like me, I work for Indiana university.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.310
It's a state, you're all paying my salary
and taxes.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.850
You're all paying my health insurance.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:42.320
Even though I have private health insurance.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.340
It's public taxpayer money, which is paying
for it.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.750
And Charlie, when you add all that up, it's
like two thirds of healthcare spending is

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.340
already publicly finance in the United States.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:55.420
That's almost more per person than any other
country pays total.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.870
And then on top of that, we still feel like
we're all privately paying way too much money

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:03.170
for premiums that our jobs and even on top
of that, we feel like a pen, way too much

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:07.050
money out of pocket when it comes to copays
and deductibles.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:12.690
So no matter which way you want to measure
how we're spending, what the cost of care

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:15.970
is in the United States, it's, it's just unbelievably
high.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:19.709
Especially, I mean, Uber would be the first
person to tell you, um, you know, rest his

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:20.709
soul.

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:21.709
But, but it's the prices.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:27.270
It's, it's not even just how much are we consuming,
but it costs more per unit in the United States

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.210
than anywhere else you could possibly imagine.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:34.599
And so our costs are just through the roof
and as, and of course everybody wants that

00:04:34.599 --> 00:04:41.349
to be less or lower, but you can't do that
without sacrificing access or quality likely

00:04:41.349 --> 00:04:42.349
in some way.

00:04:42.349 --> 00:04:46.770
So let's get into this again, because it's
so much of our economy and if the drastically

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:51.210
reduced healthcare spending tomorrow, we would
instantly be in recession and or a depression.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.210
Yeah.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:56.710
So, so that's another really interesting thing
from Reinhardt's book where he says, if you

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:03.550
actually read, you know, spending in healthcare
is jobs in a very practical sense.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:09.900
And we have this idea that if we reduce spending
but get rid of extraneous things like medical

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.759
billing and insurance fraud investigations,
that it's all okay.

00:05:12.759 --> 00:05:16.960
Some people will have to find new jobs, but
there is a very direct link between total

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:18.580
healthcare spending and employment.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:19.580
Oh, Oh, well.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:24.979
Again, it's like we're not, when we say waste,
it's not money that we put in a pile and burn.

00:05:24.979 --> 00:05:27.840
Um, all of that waste is another person's
income.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:34.020
It's, it's, you know, it's paying for stuff
and people and jobs and not even just, you

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:35.020
know, drugs.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:38.169
But the people that work at the drug company,
the people that clean the offices that the

00:05:38.169 --> 00:05:43.430
drug company, the people that pave the lots
outside of the drug, it's, it's 16, 17, 18%

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.729
of GDP in the United States.

00:05:45.729 --> 00:05:50.889
Meaning about what one seventh, one eighth
of our economy is healthcare.

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:53.990
If you say tomorrow, let's instead of spending
18%, let's spend 16%.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:56.909
We just dropped GDP by two percentage points.

00:05:56.909 --> 00:05:59.430
We ended up depression.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:00.430
Those lines.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:06.030
My, you know, I am, uh, I believe Medicare
for all would be a much better system than

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.240
the one we have.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:13.129
At the same time, I have looked into enough
of the specific propo proposals that I see

00:06:13.129 --> 00:06:16.259
what may be a problem with some of the plans
for how to pay for it.

00:06:16.259 --> 00:06:19.440
And I want to see if you agree with me or
where maybe I'm getting this wrong.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:26.559
It seems to me that most of these plans assume
in order to make the numbers work a drastic

00:06:26.559 --> 00:06:30.080
reduction reduction in healthcare spending
per capita in the United States.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:34.659
That would put us, depending on the plan that
you look at in line with maybe Canada or Australia

00:06:34.659 --> 00:06:39.689
and I have not seen a good defense of how
that's going to happen.

00:06:39.689 --> 00:06:42.589
Aside from whether it would be good or bad,
we can debate that part.

00:06:42.589 --> 00:06:47.979
I understand that if you eliminate some of
the quote waste from the multi insurance billing

00:06:47.979 --> 00:06:51.330
system in different things, you'll reduce
cost a little bit, but I don't see how it

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.039
gets reduced so much.

00:06:53.039 --> 00:06:57.139
Is that a fair critique of some of the plans
for how to pay for it or am I maybe missing

00:06:57.139 --> 00:06:58.139
something

00:06:58.139 --> 00:06:59.139
of some of it?

00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:02.699
Yes, but I would say it's even, it's even
a little more complicated than that because

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:05.839
again, I would turn up, I've been in terms
of trade offs like we were at the beginning.

00:07:05.839 --> 00:07:09.529
So single payer healthcare, which is what
Medicare for all is a perfectly viable way

00:07:09.529 --> 00:07:11.180
to improve access.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:12.180
That's what it would do.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:13.180
It would get us to universal.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:14.669
So at that point they'd say, well, what's
the trade off?

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:17.460
Are we going to sacrifice quality or are we
going to sacrifice?

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:24.810
We're going to spend more money and we could
absolutely spend more money and then everything

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:25.810
would be fine.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.259
But if we're not, then we have to say, well
then what will the trade off be if we're going

00:07:28.259 --> 00:07:32.749
to significantly reduce spending at the same
time that we're massively increasing access

00:07:32.749 --> 00:07:35.110
to imagine the quality has to suffer in some
way.

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:37.710
But the broader picture is exactly what you
said.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:41.369
Like there's no magic to this single payer.

00:07:41.369 --> 00:07:43.719
Healthcare systems are not inherently cheap.

00:07:43.719 --> 00:07:49.689
They're cheap because they can use their single
payer market power to force every provider

00:07:49.689 --> 00:07:53.780
and purveyor of healthcare to accept lower
reimbursement.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.669
Um, and so that's where the magic comes in.

00:07:56.669 --> 00:08:03.389
Medicare often reimburses less than private
insurance because they just have all the patients

00:08:03.389 --> 00:08:07.020
and they can turn to providers and say, we've
got all these patients.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:11.229
If you want to make a living, you have to
accept the insurance the way that is.

00:08:11.229 --> 00:08:14.999
Theoretically, if everyone was on Medicare,
then the governor would have enormous power

00:08:14.999 --> 00:08:18.979
to say, everyone's going to have to accept
less reimbursement.

00:08:18.979 --> 00:08:20.959
That's how it's all going to function.

00:08:20.959 --> 00:08:25.860
Well there are trade offs to that, be it their
innovation, be it through what people are

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.849
willing or able to provide.

00:08:27.849 --> 00:08:32.250
The idea that everyone is going to continue
to operate at peak performance for less money

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:33.970
is not realistic.

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:38.250
And again, we can say, Oh well we'll just
take it out of fraud and waste.

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.560
That's still somebody's income.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:41.970
That's still somebody working.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:43.270
There's no magic solution.

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:49.870
So Medicare for all is a perfectly viable
way to achieve universal coverage and theoretically

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:54.230
to put use market power to reduce overall
healthcare spending.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:55.730
But there's got to be trade offs.

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:59.950
So now a defender of the Medicare for all
system would hear you talking about that and

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:02.850
say, well, there's a combination of things
there that would go into this.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:08.710
On the one hand, uh, there is a stimulative
even though, yes, a reduction in total cost

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:14.740
is a reduction in jobs, the stimulative effect
for all of the end users who no longer are

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:16.990
spending as much money out of pocket.

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:23.260
If you believe those estimates and also are
not at risk of a bankruptcy because of unpredictable

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:27.570
healthcare spending, all of that money is
going to have a much more stimulative effect

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:32.960
being spent at businesses, for example, that
will increase government revenues and therefore

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:38.840
it's all sort of going to work out in the
end in a way that is relatively neat and tidy.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:39.910
Or what do you think about that?

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:43.240
I think it will work out at the end, but I
think it's because Americans have no appetite

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:44.820
for cost control.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:48.590
I think what would happen is that we'd Pat,
if we pass a Medicare for all, what would

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:51.190
happen is the system would just cost much,
much, much more money.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:54.740
Um, because we could theoretically use this
market power that we have in Medicare right

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:58.750
now to curtail or hold down Medicare spending
